  "codebook": {
    "clarification": "Code 1 when ANY of the following are present: (1) The senator's office announces particularized federal benefits for their state or communities - grants, appropriations, disaster declarations, new or saved military missions/bases, expanded federal services, federal purchases benefiting state producers - even if an agency formally made the award and even without the literal words 'I secured it,' because issuing the announcement publicly associates the senator with the benefit. (2) Explicit responsibility language: 'secured,' 'won,' 'delivered,' 'helped secure,' 'facilitated,' 'worked to expedite,' 'played a key role,' 'at my request,' 'following my appeal/letter.' (3) The senator claims authorship or sponsorship of legislation, amendments, or provisions that have passed a chamber, been adopted, been enacted into law, or been included in an advancing legislative vehicle (committee-passed bills, leadership packages, passed appropriations or budget bills). (4) A favorable agency decision is presented as following the senator's request, letter, appeal, recommendation, nomination, or pressure (implied causation). (5) Announcement of a federal appointment of a candidate the senator recommended. Shared credit (delegations, bipartisan pairs) still counts.",
    "definition": "Credit-claiming occurs when a senator asserts, or clearly implies, personal or shared responsibility for a concrete governmental benefit or favorable governmental action that has been secured, delivered, enacted, or decided. The core message is 'I (or my office/delegation) helped cause this good thing to happen': federal dollars or projects obtained for the state, designations or disaster declarations secured, military missions gained or saved, favorable agency decisions that followed the senator's intervention, or legislative accomplishments such as authored bills, amendments, or provisions that passed, were enacted, or were included in advancing legislation.",
    "negative_clarification": "Code 0 when the text is: (1) an introduction or cosponsorship of pending legislation, a floor statement, or hearing testimony advocating a bill - position-taking; this holds even when such releases recount the senator's past efforts or record as supporting context, because the classification reflects the release's primary purpose; (2) statements of support or opposition, explanations of votes, or criticism of the administration, agencies, foreign actors, or opposing politicians; (3) commemorations, condolences, tributes, awards given to others, or awards received from outside groups for one's voting record; (4) announcements of events - town meetings, hearings, markups, investigations, contests - or of meetings with officials where no outcome is claimed; (5) 'welcoming,' 'applauding,' or 'urging' prospective or not-yet-final agency action without claiming responsibility for a delivered outcome; (6) requests, demands, or calls for action where no benefit has yet been delivered.",
    "negative_examples": [
      {
        "document_id": "57",
        "explanation": "Kennedy introduces stem-cell/anti-cloning legislation. Promoting a pending bill is position-taking: the benefits described are prospective and nothing has been enacted or delivered, so there is no claim of responsibility for a governmental outcome."
      },
      {
        "document_id": "3659",
        "explanation": "Clinton 'welcomes' an advisory board recommendation that still requires the Secretary's approval and 'urges' him to act. The benefit is prospective and she claims no responsibility for the decision - advocating future action is not credit-claiming."
      },
      {
        "document_id": "918",
        "explanation": "Allard announces a town meeting. A pure event announcement contains no claim of responsibility for any governmental benefit or action."
      }
    ],
    "positive_examples": [
      {
        "document_id": "2627",
        "explanation": "Headlined 'Sen. Talent secures $1.5 million,' the release announces that 'the Senate has approved his request' for funding for a University of Missouri research center - an explicit claim of obtaining federal dollars for his state."
      },
      {
        "document_id": "4508",
        "explanation": "Lott announces FEMA is moving trailers and mobile homes to Mississippi 'following the Senator's Monday appeal to President Bush' - the juxtaposition implies his intervention caused the delivered benefit, a credit claim without the literal words 'I secured.'"
      },
      {
        "document_id": "2318",
        "explanation": "Even inside a letter whose main purpose is urging Illinois officials to act, Durbin explicitly claims 'I helped secure an expedited environmental review' and 'facilitated the return of the project to the national priority list for federal funding' - explicit responsibility language makes the text credit-claiming despite its advocacy framing."
      }
    ]
  }
}
